      { id: 'c07', unit: 3, type: 'short', prompt: 'Distinguish peristalsis from segmentation in one sentence.', answerText: 'Peristalsis mainly propels contents along the GI tract, whereas segmentation mainly mixes contents with secretions and the absorptive surface.', rationale: 'Both are smooth-muscle patterns, but their dominant functions differ.' },

      { id: 'c08', unit: 4, type: 'mc', prompt: 'Which process makes new glucose from precursors such as lactate, glycerol and glucogenic amino acids?', options: ['Glycolysis', 'Glycogenesis', 'Glycogenolysis', 'Gluconeogenesis'], answer: 3, answerText: 'Gluconeogenesis', rationale: 'Glycogenesis stores glucose as glycogen; glycogenolysis releases glucose units from glycogen; gluconeogenesis makes new glucose.' },
      { id: 'c09', unit: 4, type: 'tf', prompt: 'Muscle glycogen directly maintains blood glucose for the rest of the body in the same way as liver glycogen.', options: ['True', 'False'], answer: 1, answerText: 'False', rationale: 'Muscle uses its glycogen mainly as local fuel; liver glycogen is positioned to support blood glucose.' },

      { id: 'c10', unit: 5, type: 'mc', prompt: 'What is a limiting amino acid?', options: ['Any amino acid that can be stored', 'The essential amino acid in shortest supply relative to need', 'The amino acid used only for energy', 'Any nonessential amino acid'], answer: 1, answerText: 'The essential amino acid in shortest supply relative to need', rationale: 'It limits protein synthesis when the other essential amino acids are available in greater relative amounts.' },
      { id: 'c11', unit: 5, type: 'short', prompt: 'Distinguish transcription from translation.', answerText: 'Transcription copies DNA information into RNA; translation uses the mRNA sequence at the ribosome to assemble an amino-acid chain.', rationale: 'Keeping the information step separate from the assembly step prevents a common terminology mix-up.' },

      { id: 'c12', unit: 6, type: 'tf', prompt: 'An 18:2 fatty acid contains 18 carbons and two carbon–carbon double bonds, so it is polyunsaturated.', options: ['True', 'False'], answer: 0, answerText: 'True', rationale: 'Two or more carbon–carbon double bonds define a polyunsaturated fatty acid.' },
      { id: 'c13', unit: 6, type: 'short', prompt: 'Give the main transport job of chylomicrons, VLDL, LDL and HDL.', answerText: 'Chylomicrons carry dietary lipid from the intestine; VLDL exports liver-made triglyceride; LDL delivers cholesterol-rich cargo to tissues; HDL participates in reverse cholesterol transport and lipid exchange.', rationale: 'The particles overlap in cargo, but their origins and dominant transport roles differ.' },

      { id: 'c14', unit: 7, type: 'mc', prompt: 'What is the direct purpose of an enzyme in a metabolic reaction?', options: ['Create energy', 'Lower activation energy', 'Change the reaction equilibrium', 'Become the final product'], answer: 1, answerText: 'Lower activation energy', rationale: 'Enzymes accelerate reactions without being consumed or creating energy.' },
      { id: 'c15', unit: 7, type: 'tf', prompt: 'NADH and FADH2 carry high-energy electrons to the respiratory chain.', options: ['True', 'False'], answer: 0, answerText: 'True', rationale: 'Their oxidation supplies electrons that help build the proton gradient used for ATP synthesis.' },

      { id: 'c16', unit: 8, type: 'mc', prompt: 'For many sedentary adults, which component is usually the largest part of total daily energy expenditure?', options: ['Thermic effect of food', 'Basal metabolic rate', 'Structured exercise only', 'Adaptive thermogenesis only'], answer: 1, answerText: 'Basal metabolic rate', rationale: 'BMR supports essential functions continuously and is often the largest component, though proportions vary.' },
      { id: 'c17', unit: 8, type: 'short', prompt: 'Why is the “3,500 kcal equals one pound” rule not an exact weight-loss prediction?', answerText: 'It is a static approximation. Energy expenditure, water, glycogen, appetite, movement and the composition of lost tissue change over time.', rationale: 'Dynamic adaptation means the same planned deficit does not produce a perfectly linear result.' },

      { id: 'c18', unit: 9, type: 'tf', prompt: 'All three energy systems contribute during exercise, although their relative contribution changes with intensity and duration.', options: ['True', 'False'], answer: 0, answerText: 'True', rationale: 'ATP-PC dominates the shortest efforts, anaerobic glycolysis supports short intense work, and oxidative metabolism has high capacity for longer activity.' },
      { id: 'c19', unit: 9, type: 'short', prompt: 'Order the three ATP systems from fastest/lowest capacity to slower/highest capacity.', answerText: 'ATP-PC (phosphagen) → anaerobic glycolysis → oxidative metabolism.', rationale: 'The ordering links rate and capacity rather than implying that any system switches completely off.' },

      { id: 'c20', unit: 10, type: 'mc', prompt: 'Dermatitis, diarrhea and dementia are classically associated with severe deficiency of which vitamin?', options: ['Thiamin', 'Riboflavin', 'Niacin', 'Biotin'], answer: 2, answerText: 'Niacin', rationale: 'Pellagra is the classic severe niacin-deficiency syndrome; death is the traditional fourth D.' },
      { id: 'c21', unit: 10, type: 'tf', prompt: 'FAD and FMN are riboflavin-derived electron carriers.', options: ['True', 'False'], answer: 0, answerText: 'True', rationale: 'Riboflavin is the vitamin precursor for these redox-active cofactors.' },

      { id: 'c22', unit: 11, type: 'mc', prompt: 'Which sequence correctly describes vitamin D activation?', options: ['Kidney makes 25(OH)D, then liver makes 1,25(OH)2D', 'Liver makes 25(OH)D, then kidney mainly makes 1,25(OH)2D', 'Intestine makes D3, then liver makes D2', 'Bone makes 25(OH)D, then skin makes 1,25(OH)2D'], answer: 1, answerText: 'Liver makes 25(OH)D, then kidney mainly makes 1,25(OH)2D', rationale: 'The two-step sequence is a high-yield distinction between the major circulating status marker and the active hormone.' },
      { id: 'c23', unit: 11, type: 'tf', prompt: 'High supplemental vitamin E intake can increase bleeding risk.', options: ['True', 'False'], answer: 0, answerText: 'True', rationale: 'Excess vitamin E can interfere with clotting; “antioxidant” does not mean unlimited intake is harmless.' },
      { id: 'c24', unit: 11, type: 'short', prompt: 'Contrast the cancer links for HPV, hepatitis viruses and high-temperature meat cooking.', answerText: 'Persistent high-risk HPV causes nearly all cervical cancers; chronic HBV or HCV can cause liver cancer; high-temperature meat cooking can form HCAs and smoke can add PAHs, which are exposure-related hazards rather than viruses.', rationale: 'The mechanisms differ: persistent infection versus chemical exposure.' },

      { id: 'c25', unit: 12, type: 'mc', prompt: 'Which pair correctly matches the major cation with its primary fluid compartment?', options: ['Sodium intracellular; potassium extracellular', 'Sodium extracellular; potassium intracellular', 'Both mainly extracellular', 'Both mainly intracellular'], answer: 1, answerText: 'Sodium extracellular; potassium intracellular', rationale: 'The unequal distribution is central to membrane potentials, fluid balance and transport.' },
      { id: 'c26', unit: 12, type: 'short', prompt: 'Give one key distinction between aldosterone and ADH.', answerText: 'Aldosterone primarily promotes renal sodium reabsorption (with potassium secretion), whereas ADH primarily increases renal water reabsorption.', rationale: 'Sodium regulation and water regulation interact but are not the same signal.' },

      { id: 'c27', unit: 13, type: 'tf', prompt: 'Vitamin C consumed with a meal can enhance nonheme iron absorption.', options: ['True', 'False'], answer: 0, answerText: 'True', rationale: 'Vitamin C helps maintain iron in a more absorbable form and can counter some meal inhibitors.' },
      { id: 'c28', unit: 13, type: 'short', prompt: 'Distinguish transferrin, ferritin and myoglobin.', answerText: 'Transferrin transports iron in plasma; ferritin is the main intracellular storage protein; myoglobin binds oxygen in muscle.', rationale: 'The three proteins connect iron with transport, storage and muscle function.' },

      { id: 'c29', unit: 14, type: 'mc', prompt: 'Which definition is correct?', options: ['Preterm birth means birth weight below 2,500 g', 'Low birth weight means delivery before 37 weeks', 'Preterm birth means delivery before 37 completed weeks', 'Low birth weight depends on gestational age only'], answer: 2, answerText: 'Preterm birth means delivery before 37 completed weeks', rationale: 'Gestational age and birth weight are separate measures; low birth weight is below 2,500 g regardless of gestational age.' },
      { id: 'c30', unit: 14, type: 'tf', prompt: 'Hyperplasia means an increase in cell number, while hypertrophy means an increase in cell size.', options: ['True', 'False'], answer: 0, answerText: 'True', rationale: 'The terms describe different mechanisms of growth.' }
